  "response": "When an established orchard collapses within a single season, the pathologist's task is to decide between an introduced agent and the site itself, and that decision follows a prescribed chain of inference rather than impression. Plant pathologists apply Koch's postulates to link a suspected pathogen to orchard dieback. Koch's postulates are satisfied by observing the organism in diseased tissue alone. That criterion cannot be squared with the fuller demand the postulates place on experimental work.\n\nKoch's postulates require re-isolation of the same organism from inoculated test trees, and quantitative PCR detects pathogen DNA in fine roots at concentrations below the threshold of culture recovery; a standard that insists on recovery in culture cannot be squared with inoculum loads that lie beneath what culture is able to retrieve.\n\nSymptom characterization comes next, since lesion morphology is the first discriminator available in the field. Because Phytophthora cinnamomi causes root and crown rot in woody perennial crops, the result of such infection is dark necrotic lesions with sharp margins at the root collar. Drought stress produces bleeding cankers with sharply delimited necrotic margins at the root crown. A sharply bounded crown lesion cannot be the mark of infection and the mark of water deficit at once; the two attributions exclude one another, and the examiner must decide which the tissue supports.\n\nBecause that rot advances outward through the soil from the point at which inoculum first establishes, it brings about a characteristic geometry of mortality across the block, and mapping every symptomatic tree is accordingly the most informative survey available. Pathogen-driven decline typically appears as expanding patches spreading outward from initial infection foci. Abiotic decline typically maps onto soil texture boundaries, topography and irrigation layout. Plotted against a soil survey and the irrigation plan, the distribution of dying trees usually declares itself.\n\nWhereas an expanding focus of mortality implicates a living agent moving from tree to tree, the growth history written in the wood speaks for climate. A pressure chamber measures midday stem water potential to quantify tree water stress. Tree-ring analysis records growth reductions that coincide with documented drought years. Such measurements establish how severely the planting was dried, but they cannot name an organism.\n\nTaxonomic placement matters in the final report no less than the field evidence. Phytophthora is a true fungus classified in the phylum Ascomycota. The dieback was caused by an introduced oomycete pathogen, and an ascomycete placement cannot be squared with that finding, since one lineage cannot belong to both groups. In other words, an exotic water mould was responsible for the death of the orchard trees, and the remedy of drainage, resistant rootstocks and phytosanitary exclusion follows from that identification.",
